// THE_SIMPLIFIED_PLAN_v4.0 — content, part 2 of 3 (Parts 5 to 8)
module.exports = [

// =========================================================================
{t:'H1', text:'Part 5  ·  Data preparation'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['Why this part is the longest',
  'Data preparation is where most remote-sensing studies actually fail, and the failures are quiet. A processing error usually announces itself — values go out of range, a map looks wrong. A preparation error usually does not: every number in the table stays plausible while the sample size, the weighting or the meaning of a column has silently changed. What follows is the full sequence, in the order you run it.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'5.1  The preparation sequence at a glance'},
{t:'TBL', header:['','Step','What it does'],
 rows:[
 ['DP1','Cloud, shadow, snow and water masking','Removes observations that are not of the land surface.'],
 ['DP2','Topographic illumination correction','Removes the effect of slope aspect on reflectance.'],
 ['DP3','Built-up masking','Separates the CONDITION measurement from the COMPOSITION measurement.'],
 ['DP4','Monthly compositing','Turns irregular observations into a regular twelve-month series.'],
 ['DP5','Vegetation-year alignment','Puts every site’s growing season in the same place in its own year.'],
 ['DP6','Annual metric computation','Reduces twelve monthly values to the annual metrics of Part 3.'],
 ['DP7','Quality screening','Decides which site-years are good enough to keep.'],
 ['DP8','Panel assembly','Joins five separate products into one analysis table.'],
 ['DP9','Integrity checks','Confirms the join did not silently change the data.'],
 ['DP10','Outlier screening','Finds extreme values and decides what each one is.'],
 ['DP11','Missing-data rules','Fixes what happens when something is absent.'],
 ['DP12','Freeze and version the analysis table','Marks the end of preparation and the start of analysis.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'5.2  DP1 — Cloud, shadow, snow and water masking'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Link each Sentinel-2 scene to its Cloud Score+ record and keep only pixels whose clearness score is at least 0.60. This threshold comes from the product’s own reference implementation; 0.50 and 0.70 are run later as sensitivity check SC9.',
 'Mask permanent water using the surface-water occurrence layer at a 50% threshold. Water in a vegetation index is not low vegetation; it is not vegetation at all, and leaving it in depresses every metric at lakeside sites.',
 'Exclude snow and ice by the same clearness score, with a visual check at high-latitude and high-elevation sites in winter months.',
 'Do NOT use the legacy quality bands shipped with the imagery. They miss thin cloud and — more damagingly — cloud shadow, which depresses vegetation indices without being visually obvious. They also changed availability partway through the study window, which would introduce a second, independent discontinuity on top of the radiometric one.',
]},
{t:'P', text:'One thing masking does NOT solve, and you should state it in the thesis. Masking removes cloudy observations. It does not remove the fact that cloudy days went unobserved. In persistently cloudy regions the surviving observations are the subset of days that happened to be clear — and clear days in a humid tropical climate are systematically drier and sunnier than average. A composite built from them describes a conditional state, not the annual state. The pilot measured how uneven this is: a median of 70 clear observations per site-year in temperate zones against 32 in the tropics, with a minimum of 7. Carry n_clear as a covariate and report it by climate group.'},

{t:'H2', text:'5.3  DP2 — Topographic illumination correction'},
{t:'P', text:'On sloping ground the light reaching the surface depends on the angle between the slope and the sun. A north-facing slope in the northern hemisphere receives less direct light than a south-facing one at the same latitude on the same day, and every index built from reflectance differs accordingly.'},
{t:'P', text:'This matters here specifically. Intentional communities are disproportionately founded on marginal land, and marginal land is disproportionately sloping. A settlement–control difference driven by slope aspect would be an artefact aligned with the study’s central comparison.'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Compute the illumination condition from the elevation model and the per-scene solar geometry.',
 'Apply a sun–canopy–sensor correction with an empirical moderating term. Purely cosine-based corrections over-correct weakly lit slopes and produce implausibly bright shadowed terrain.',
 'VERIFY IT WORKED: after correction, compute the correlation between each index and the illumination condition across pixels within a stratum. It should be near zero. If a material correlation remains, the correction has not worked and must be revised before you proceed. Run this per stratum, not globally — correction performance varies with terrain and latitude.',
]},

{t:'H2', text:'5.4  DP3 — Built-up masking, and the two forms of every measurement'},
{t:'P', text:'The pilot measured that a conventional village zone at 150 m is on average 44% built surface, with a standard deviation of 18 percentage points across sites. An index computed over that zone without separating the two answers neither of two distinct questions cleanly: is there MORE vegetation, and is the vegetation that exists BETTER?'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Form','What it measures','How it is computed'],
 rows:[
 ['CONDITION form','How good the vegetation that exists is','The fourteen condition metrics computed over VEGETATED pixels only, with built-up pixels masked. This is VCI.'],
 ['COMPOSITION form','How much of the zone is vegetated, and in what classes','Land-cover fractions over the FULL zone, unmasked. This is LCC. Reported as a result in its own right, never rescaled into VCI.'],
 ['Both together','The full picture','Reported side by side. "More vegetation AND better vegetation" is a stronger finding than either alone; "more but not better" and "better but not more" are informative outcomes, not failures.'],
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Four rules for the mask'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Take built-up probability from the land-cover PROBABILITY composite, not from a hard label, so the threshold is explicit and adjustable. Mask pixels whose annual mean built probability exceeds 0.50; sensitivity check SC10 varies this.',
 'APPLY THE MASK BEFORE computing any bare-ground, bare-soil or disturbance quantity. A sealed roof, a road and a yard are spectrally close to bare soil, so an unmasked bare-ground fraction counts infrastructure as exposed earth — at exactly the sites with the most infrastructure, which inverts the meaning of metric VM6 and of flag component FC3.',
 'Apply the mask IDENTICALLY at reference sites. A reference level computed on unmasked land sits on a different scale from a masked study value, and every rescaled score would be biased.',
 'Record the masked fraction per site per zone per year and report it. It IS the composition result, so it is never discarded as a processing detail.',
]},
{t:'P', text:'PCI is the exception: the three provisioning metrics are computed over the FULL zone, unmasked, because managed productive area fraction is definitionally about the whole zone. Computing it on the masked area would make it a ratio of vegetated land to vegetated land.'},
{t:'P', text:'One consequence to state in the thesis rather than conceal. VCI is measured over vegetated pixels and PCI over the full zone, so the two outcomes sit on slightly different spatial supports. This is deliberate — each is measured where it is meaningful — and the masked fraction is reported per site so a reader can see where it matters most.'},

{t:'H2', text:'5.5  DP4 and DP5 — Monthly compositing and vegetation-year alignment'},
{t:'H3', text:'Monthly composites'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'For each site, each zone and each month of the vegetation year, take the MEDIAN of all clear observations in that month.',
 'Use the median rather than the mean throughout. Residual undetected cloud and shadow produce one-sided outliers that a mean absorbs and a median resists.',
 'Where a month has no clear observations, leave it EMPTY. Do not fill it with a placeholder value, do not interpolate from neighbouring months, and do not carry the previous month forward. Record how many months have data as n_months.',
 'Compute indices from the composited reflectance bands, not by compositing per-scene index values.',
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Vegetation years'},
{t:'P', text:'A calendar year works in the northern hemisphere and breaks in the southern, where the growing season straddles the year boundary. A calendar year at an Australian or Chilean site contains the tail of one season and the head of the next, with winter in the middle — which makes seasonal amplitude, dry-season minimum and green-up timing meaningless there.'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Where the site is','Vegetation year runs','Why'],
 rows:[
 ['Above 23.5° N','1 January – 31 December','The growing season already sits inside the calendar year.'],
 ['Below 23.5° S','1 July – 30 June','Puts the austral growing season in the middle and winter at the edges, mirroring the northern case.'],
 ['Between the tropics','Twelve months beginning at the site’s climatologically driest month','Tropical phenology follows rainfall, not temperature. Take the driest month from long-term CHIRPS climatology over at least twenty years, so it reflects climate rather than the particular years of the study.'],
]},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Fix each site’s start month ONCE and never recompute it. A start month that shifts between years makes years incomparable within a site.',
 'Where a tropical site has two rainfall minima, use the deeper one and record the site as bimodal.',
 'Where a site is aseasonal — no month below a defined fraction of the annual mean — set the vegetation year to the calendar year and mark its seasonal metrics UNINFORMATIVE rather than computing and quietly using them.',
]},

{t:'H2', text:'5.6  DP6 and DP7 — Annual metrics and quality screening'},
{t:'H3', text:'Computing the annual metrics'},
{t:'P', text:'Each of the twelve monthly composites feeds the annual metrics of §3.3. Take the metrics from the FITTED monthly series rather than from the raw observations where a choice exists: a within-year range statistic computed from raw observations rises mechanically with the number of observations sampled, so a well-observed site would appear more variable than a poorly observed one for no physical reason.'},
{t:'H3', text:'The three quality gates'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Gate','Threshold','What happens if it fails'],
 rows:[
 ['Clear observations per site-year','At least 12, spread across at least 6 different months','The site-year is marked MISSING. The site keeps its other years. A site failing in four or more of seven years is excluded from the primary analysis and reported.'],
 ['Valid pixels per zone per year','At least 30 pixels after all masking','That zone-year is marked MISSING. THIS MATTERS MOST FOR RING 1, which spans 0–50 m over the built core and may have almost no vegetated pixels once masked. It also binds on the smallest polygons: a 1.0 ha polygon holds 100 pixels before masking and roughly 56 after, which clears the floor but not comfortably — which is why 1.0 ha is the minimum and smaller sites fall back to a 75 m circle.'],
 ['Disturbance','No burned area recorded within the analysis extent during the study window','The site is FLAGGED, not automatically dropped. Sensitivity check SC8 excludes flagged sites. A disturbed site does not represent its own management, but the decision to exclude belongs to the analysis, not the preparation.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'Note what the second gate protects against. Without it, a ring containing four vegetated pixels would produce a number that looks exactly like a number from a ring containing four hundred, and the radial gradient would be noisiest exactly where the effect is expected to be largest.'},
{t:'P', text:'An extra gate for the longitudinal cohort. A cohort site is admitted to analysis A7 only if it passes the observation gate in AT LEAST TWO of its three pre-onset years AND at least two of its four post-onset years. A site with one usable pre-year cannot support a before-and-after comparison, and including it would let a single noisy year masquerade as a baseline. Report how many cohort sites this excludes, and check whether the exclusions cluster by climate group. If the tropics lose disproportionately many — which is likely, given a median of 32 clear observations per site-year there against 70 in temperate zones — then the cohort result applies mainly to temperate and continental communities, and the thesis says so.'},

{t:'H2', text:'5.7  DP8 and DP9 — Panel assembly and integrity checks'},
{t:'P', text:'The analysis table is a join across five separately produced things: the site register, the quartet register, the coded community attributes (including the polygon geometry), the annual extraction table with its static companion, and the reference levels. Each is fine on its own. The JOIN is what needs checking.'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['Why a join error is more dangerous than a processing error',
  'The characteristic failure is a many-to-many join where a many-to-one was intended. It corrupts no value — every number stays correct — it simply repeats rows. The duplicated sites then carry more weight than they should, standard errors shrink because the apparent sample is larger than the real one, and every value-based check passes cleanly. The opposite failure is equally quiet: a key mismatch drops rows, and sites disappear without anything registering their absence. If the mismatch correlates with something — a naming convention used in one country, a missing attribute at one class of site — the loss is not random. Neither is detectable from the values. Both are trivially detectable from row counts.'],
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Join order'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Annual extraction table ← static table, on site_id and zone. Many-to-one.',
 'Result ← site register, on site_id. Many-to-one.',
 'Result ← quartet register, on quartet_id. Many-to-one. Reference sites carry a null quartet_id BY DESIGN; that null is expected, not an orphan.',
 'Result ← coded community attributes, on site_id. Many-to-one, settlements only. Controls inherit their settlement’s onset and polygon through the quartet, never through a direct join.',
 'Reference levels applied as a LOOKUP keyed on stratum AND reference circle, not as a join, so that rescaling cannot expand rows.',
]},
{t:'H3', text:'The six checks'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Check','Rule'],
 rows:[
 ['Row-count invariance','Record the row count before and after every join. It must be IDENTICAL. Any change, in either direction, stops assembly. No join in this pipeline is allowed to change the number of rows.'],
 ['Expected total','Compute the expected row count arithmetically BEFORE opening the extraction output, using the derivation in §4.4 and no other: 848 × 7 × 7, plus 1,350 × 5 × 7, plus 260 × 7 × 9 = 105,182. Compare. A discrepancy is investigated, never resolved by filtering the table down to the expected size. The SC1 equal-area export is a separate table with its own expected total of 8,276.'],
 ['No duplicate keys','No repeated combination of site_id, zone and veg_year. Zero tolerated. This is the check that catches a many-to-many join.'],
 ['No orphans either way','No extraction row without a site record; no registered site without extraction rows — unless the absence is recorded with a reason.'],
 ['Quartet completeness','Every quartet has exactly one settlement and three controls, and all four carry the same polygon shape and area.'],
 ['Column sanity','Every column has the type, unit and plausible range you expect. Areas in hectares, distances in metres, indices between −1 and 1, rescaled scores between 0 and 1, fractions between 0 and 1.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'Write these six results into a short assembly report and keep it. It is two paragraphs in the thesis and it is what allows a reader to believe the rest.'},

{t:'H2', text:'5.8  DP10 — Outlier screening'},
{t:'P', text:'Flag and investigate. Never silently remove. Trimming an extreme value from an outcome variable is selection on the outcome. A settlement scoring unusually well or unusually badly is exactly the kind of observation this study exists to find, and a procedure that deletes it for being unusual would discard the finding while appearing to clean the data.'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'For each metric within each stratum, compute a robust standardised deviation using the MEDIAN and the median absolute deviation, not the mean and standard deviation. The mean and standard deviation are themselves distorted by the outliers you are looking for.',
 'Flag observations more than 5 robust standard deviations from the stratum median.',
 'Inspect every flagged observation on high-resolution imagery, against its own seven-year series, and against the notable_context field coded in Stage 1.',
 'Resolve each to one of three recorded outcomes: a DATA ERROR, corrected at its source and the table rebuilt; an UNDETECTED DISTURBANCE, handled as a disturbance flag; or a GENUINE EXTREME VALUE, retained unchanged.',
 'Quantify the influence of retained extremes: re-run the primary analysis with each flagged site removed in turn, and report the largest change any single site produces. A result that depends on one site is reported as depending on one site.',
]},
{t:'P', text:'Do this before you look at the settlement–control contrast, on all sites at once. Screening outliers after seeing the contrast would let inconvenient settlements be scrutinised harder than convenient ones, without any conscious intent.'},

{t:'H2', text:'5.9  DP11 — Missing-data rules'},
{t:'P', text:'Do not impute outcome values. Missingness here is not random: it follows cloud cover, which follows climate; and observation adequacy, which follows latitude. Imputing outcomes would move information between sites along exactly the axes the study stratifies on.'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Situation','Rule'],
 rows:[
 ['A site-year fails the observation gate','Marked missing. The site contributes its remaining years. Sites failing in four or more of seven years leave the primary analysis and are reported.'],
 ['A zone-year has under 30 valid pixels','That zone-year is missing. Most often ring 1 at dense settlements.'],
 ['One metric missing within a dimension','The dimension score is computed from the remaining metrics, and the number contributing is recorded.'],
 ['A whole dimension missing','VCI is computed from the remaining dimensions and FLAGGED as partial. A site missing two or more dimensions is excluded from the primary analysis.'],
 ['Onset year unavailable, or flagged as a founding-year proxy','The site is excluded from analysis A4 only, and cannot enter the cohort. It stays in every other analysis.'],
 ['Documentary managed area unavailable','area_agreement_tier is B, NOT blank, and the site is excluded from SC16. The polygon gives it full geometry and it stays in every analysis. This is a complete and correct state, not a gap.'],
 ['Documentary evidence tier is Fail','The site is reported in the sample description and excluded from SC18. It stays in every primary analysis — the tier measures documentation, not eligibility.'],
 ['Seasonal metrics at an aseasonal site','Marked uninformative and excluded from the persistence dimension for that site.'],
 ['A flag component unavailable','The flag is reported as UNAVAILABLE, never as NOT RAISED. An unmeasured subsidy is not an absent one.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'Version 3.8 carried a further rule here — "a practice code not codeable excludes that community from A6 for that code only" — which is deleted with A6.'},
{t:'P', text:'Report the missingness pattern itself — per site, per year, per metric, with the reason — as a small table in the thesis. A reader cannot assess a result without knowing what is absent from it.'},

{t:'H2', text:'5.10  DP12 — Freeze the analysis table'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'When all checks pass, save the analysis table with a version number and a date, and do not overwrite it.',
 'Record in the decisions log that the table was frozen, and on what date.',
 'If you later have to rebuild it, save the new version alongside rather than replacing it, and note in the log what changed and why.',
]},
{t:'P', text:'This is the boundary between preparation and analysis. Everything before it is allowed to change freely; everything after it is measurement.'},

// =========================================================================
{t:'H1', text:'Part 6  ·  Reference levels and the index'},
{t:'P', text:'This part turns raw metrics into comparable scores. It contains the study’s own formulas, written out.'},

{t:'H2', text:'6.1  Why rescaling is necessary'},
{t:'P', text:'A dry-season NDVI of 0.45 is exceptional in semi-arid rangeland and poor in humid tropical forest. Comparing raw values across sixty countries would measure climate, and the climate difference is far larger than any effect the study is looking for. Every metric is therefore expressed not in its own units but as a proportion of what is ATTAINABLE at that location. This requires an external answer to "what should be here?", supplied independently of the settlements themselves. Building that answer is the purpose of the reference pools.'},

{t:'H2', text:'6.2  Three reference pools, and what each anchors'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['Why provisioning and condition anchor differently',
  'Rescaling every metric against natural reference sites as the ceiling is right for condition and wrong for provisioning: a natural forest produces almost nothing harvestable, so using it as the ceiling for provisioning capacity would give nearly every settlement and control a score near 1.0 and destroy all discrimination on the second primary outcome. The two axes need different anchors, and three pools supply them symmetrically.'],
]},
{t:'TBL', header:['Pool','Size','What it is','What it anchors'],
 rows:[
 ['NATURAL','~600 sites','Least-disturbed land: human modification below threshold, no built-up, no cropland, no forest loss, no fire, away from roads','The CEILING for condition (95th percentile) and the FLOOR for provisioning (5th percentile)'],
 ['SEMI-URBAN, reference subset','~300 sites','Low-density settled land at the rural fringe','The FLOOR for condition (5th percentile)'],
 ['SEMI-URBAN, held-out subset','~150 sites','The same, drawn from the same pool but DISJOINT','Nothing. Used only as the comparison group for H6'],
 ['PRODUCTIVE','~300 sites','High-output managed farmland: cropland or managed grassland probability above threshold, no built-up, upper part of the stratum productivity distribution','The CEILING for provisioning (95th percentile)'],
]},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['Why the semi-urban pool is split',
  'If semi-urban sites were simultaneously the FLOOR of the condition scale and the COMPARISON GROUP for the density hypothesis, they would score near zero by construction — so H6 would "confirm" a difference the arithmetic created. Draw the semi-urban pool larger, then split it with a recorded seed: two-thirds set the reference floor, one-third is held out and used only for H6. The two subsets are disjoint, so the H6 comparison is no longer against the scale’s own definition. Record the seed in the decisions log. This costs one line of code and removes a circularity a reviewer would certainly find.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'Reference levels are computed FIVE TIMES per stratum, at 75, 110, 150, 210 and 300 m, and each study site is rescaled against whichever set is closest in AREA to its polygon. The reasoning is in §4.3.'},

{t:'H2', text:'6.3  Strata — where a reference level is meaningful'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Variable','Why it is needed'],
 rows:[
 ['Köppen main climate group (A, B, C, D)','Water and temperature availability set the first-order limit on attainable vegetation. Main groups rather than full classes, so strata stay populated.'],
 ['Terrestrial biome','Two places in one climate group can support entirely different vegetation — temperate grassland and temperate broadleaf forest are both group C.'],
 ['Terrain class (flat, undulating, hilly, steep)','Attainable condition on a 30-degree slope differs from that on a floodplain, and settlements are disproportionately on sloping ground.'],
]},
{t:'H3', text:'The pooling ladder'},
{t:'P', text:'Crossing all three produces many cells, and some will be thinly populated. Declare this ladder in advance and apply it automatically, so that the decision to pool is never made after seeing which pooling gives a convenient answer.'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Rung 0 — full stratum: climate group × biome × terrain class. Use where at least 30 reference sites of the relevant pool exist AT THE RELEVANT RADIUS.',
 'Rung 1 — pool terrain classes, keeping climate group × biome. Record that terrain was pooled.',
 'Rung 2 — pool to the nearest adjacent biome within the climate group.',
 'Rung 3 — pool to climate group alone.',
 'Rung 4 — no defensible reference. Sites in that stratum are reported descriptively and excluded from rescaled analyses.',
]},
{t:'P', text:'Record the rung reached for every stratum AND every radius, and report it. A reference level from rung 3 carries less authority than one from rung 0, and a reader should be able to see which is which. Note that the rung can differ between radii within one stratum, because the five reference extractions are the same sites measured at five sizes but the 30-site floor is applied independently.'},

{t:'H2', text:'6.4  The rescaling formula'},
{t:'P', text:'For metric m at site i in stratum s, at reference radius r:'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'rescaled(m,i)  =  ( raw(m,i) − LOW(m,s,r) ) / ( HIGH(m,s,r) − LOW(m,s,r) )'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'clipped to [0, 1];  r is the reference radius closest in AREA to that site’s polygon'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Term','Definition'],
 rows:[
 ['raw(m,i)','The metric as extracted, at site i, averaged across the seven vegetation years.'],
 ['HIGH(m,s,r)','For CONDITION metrics: the 95th percentile across NATURAL reference sites in stratum s at radius r. For PROVISIONING metrics: the 95th percentile across PRODUCTIVE reference sites in stratum s at radius r.'],
 ['LOW(m,s,r)','For CONDITION metrics: the 5th percentile across the SEMI-URBAN reference subset in stratum s at radius r. For PROVISIONING metrics: the 5th percentile across NATURAL reference sites in stratum s at radius r.'],
 ['Clipping','Values outside [0, 1] are set to the boundary. Record the proportion clipped at each end, per metric and stratum. A high clipping rate means the anchors are badly placed for that stratum and needs investigating.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'Why the 95th percentile and not the maximum. The maximum of a sample is unstable — it is whichever single site happens to be extreme, and it changes if you resample. The 95th percentile is stable, has an interpretable meaning (the level exceeded by one site in twenty), and can be given a confidence interval by bootstrap.'},
{t:'H3', text:'Sign inversion'},
{t:'P', text:'Three metrics run in the opposite direction: for them a LOWER raw value means BETTER condition. Invert them BEFORE rescaling, as raw_inverted = −raw. The three are VM6 bare-ground fraction, VM7 interannual variability and VM14 seasonal decline rate. Record the inversion explicitly in your code. An un-inverted metric enters the aggregate with the wrong sign, degrades the index, and produces no visible error — this is the most common silent mistake in composite index construction.'},

{t:'H2', text:'6.5  Aggregation — the index formulas'},
{t:'H3', text:'Within a dimension: arithmetic mean'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'VCI-P  =  ( r(VM1) + r(VM2) + r(VM13) ) / 3'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'VCI-S  =  ( r(VM3) + r(VM4) + r(VM5) + r(VM6) + r(VM7) + r(VM14) ) / 6'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'VCI-T  =  ( r(VM8) + r(VM9) ) / 2'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'VCI-C  =  ( r(VM10) + r(VM11) + r(VM12) ) / 3'},
{t:'P', text:'Arithmetic averaging is right WITHIN a dimension, because metrics there are alternative measurements of the same property and a low value on one is legitimately compensated by a high value on another. A site may express persistence through moisture retention or through cover duration; either is persistence.'},
{t:'P', text:'ONE EXCEPTION TO THE POLYGON, AND IT IS DELIBERATE. The three configuration metrics are computed over the COMMON 150 metre circle at every site, not over the polygon. A 70 metre pattern window covers 1.54 hectares, so a small or thin polygon would hold only one or two window-areas — far too few for a landscape pattern metric to mean anything, and the number would differ from site to site. Productivity, persistence and structure come from the polygon; configuration comes from the common circle, identically everywhere. State this in the methods rather than leaving it implicit.'},
{t:'H3', text:'Between dimensions: geometric mean'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'VCI  =  ( VCI-P × VCI-S × VCI-T × VCI-C ) ^ (1/4)'},
{t:'TBL', header:['','Site A — balanced','Site B — a plantation',''],
 rows:[
 ['Productivity','0.60','0.95',''],
 ['Persistence','0.60','0.90',''],
 ['Structure','0.60','0.95',''],
 ['Configuration','0.60','0.10',''],
 ['Arithmetic mean','0.600','0.725','B wins'],
 ['GEOMETRIC mean','0.600','0.546','A wins'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'Site B is productive, tall and persistent with almost no spatial or phenological variety. Under arithmetic averaging it beats the balanced site. Under geometric averaging it does not, because the near-zero configuration score pulls the product down and cannot be bought back by excellence elsewhere. This is the property the study needs: landscape simplification always trades a low score on one dimension for high scores on others, so an additive index would award its highest scores to exactly the landscapes the study is designed to identify as DIFFERENT from intentional communities.'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Floor every rescaled value at 0.01 before taking the geometric mean. A single true zero would annihilate the product. Report how often the floor is applied.',
 'If one dimension is entirely missing, compute the geometric mean over the remaining three and flag the site as partial. If two or more are missing, exclude the site from the primary analysis.',
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Provisioning: arithmetic mean'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'PCI  =  ( r(PM1) + r(PM2) + r(PM3) ) / 3'},
{t:'P', text:'Arithmetic, because the three provisioning metrics measure the same property — how much productive capacity the land captures — through extent, intensity and energy. There is no compensation problem to guard against here.'},
{t:'H3', text:'The management-diagnostic score'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'MDS  =  ( r(VM5) + r(VM6) + r(VM11) + r(VM12) + r(VM14) + r(PM1) + r(PM2) ) / 7'},
{t:'P', text:'A single arithmetic mean of the SEVEN management-dominated metrics. Used only in analysis A5. It is NOT a rebuilt four-dimension index — that cannot be formed, because productivity and structure contain no management-dominated metrics at all. Version 3.8 described this as six metrics in three places and seven in three others; it is seven, and every reference in this plan says so (§0.4, correction 1).'},

{t:'H2', text:'6.6  The two flags — formulas and thresholds'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'FC1 — Rainfall–NDVI coupling  =  corr( NDVI_anomaly(y), Rain_anomaly(y) )  over y = 1…7'},
{t:'P', text:'where anomaly(y) = value(y) − mean across the seven years. In a rain-fed system, a dry year gives a lower NDVI and a wet year a higher one, so the correlation is positive. Irrigation breaks that link. A LOW or negative coupling where rainfall is limiting is the first signature of imported water.'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'FC2 — Rain-use efficiency  =  mean annual kNDVI integral / mean annual rainfall'},
{t:'P', text:'Expressed as a percentile of the stratum distribution. Producing much more vegetation per millimetre than neighbours in the same climate is the second signature.'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'FC3 — Bare-soil episode frequency  =  number of months per year with mean BSI above the stratum 75th percentile'},
{t:'P', text:'Averaged across the seven years. Computed AFTER built-up masking, or roofs and roads are counted as bare soil.'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'FC4 — Transition abruptness  =  max over months of | NDVI(month+1) − NDVI(month) |'},
{t:'P', text:'Expressed as a percentile of the stratum distribution. Mechanical operations produce step changes; natural phenology is gradual.'},
{t:'TBL', header:['State','Rule'],
 rows:[
 ['RAISED','BOTH components exceed the stratum 90th percentile — or, for FC1, fall below the stratum 10th percentile.'],
 ['INDETERMINATE','Exactly one component crosses its threshold, or one component is unavailable.'],
 ['NOT RAISED','Neither component crosses its threshold.'],
 ['UNAVAILABLE','Both components are unavailable. Reported as unavailable, never as NOT RAISED.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'The two-component rule exists because each component alone has an innocent explanation — a wet run of years, a naturally sparse stratum — and their coincidence does not.'},

{t:'H2', text:'6.7  Validating the index before you use it'},
{t:'P', text:'Do this before you compute the index on any of your 212 settlements. It is the one sequencing rule this plan insists on.'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Assemble 40 to 60 landscapes whose character you know independently, spread across your climate groups, in four classes: documented monoculture plantation; documented restoration or long-established agroforestry; intact natural reference land; degraded, eroded or abandoned land.',
 'SPLIT THEM 70/30 WITH A RECORDED SEED, stratified by class and climate group. The 70% is the DEVELOPMENT set; the 30% is the HOLD-OUT set. Record the seed in the decisions log.',
 'Write down what you EXPECT the index to do for each class before running it.',
 'Run the index on the DEVELOPMENT set only. Adjust the specification freely here if it misbehaves — that is what the development set is for.',
 'When you are satisfied, run it ONCE on the hold-out set. Report that result whatever it shows, and do not adjust the specification afterwards.',
 'THE CRITICAL TEST: the plantation must score BELOW a balanced site of lower raw productivity. If it does not, the geometric mean is not doing its job and the specification needs fixing.',
 'Check the class ordering holds within each climate group separately. An index validated only in temperate Europe cannot be claimed to work in the Sahel, and the thesis should say which zones were validated.',
]},
{t:'P', text:'Why the 70/30 split is worth the extra twenty landscapes. Without it, the same landscapes both develop and validate the index, and "you tuned the index until it passed its own test" is a fair objection with no answer. With it, the hold-out result is a genuine out-of-sample check. If the hold-out result is materially worse than the development result, say so and report both: an index that orders classes correctly on development landscapes and poorly on hold-out landscapes has been over-fitted, and that is a finding about the instrument you need to know before interpreting anything else.'},
{t:'P', text:'ONE NOTE ON THE VALIDATION CLASSES. The four classes are landscapes whose character is known INDEPENDENTLY of this study’s documentary record — from published site descriptions, land-cover history or direct knowledge. They are not the 212 communities and they are not coded from the same sources. Removing the practice codes does not touch this step: the validation set never used them.'},
{t:'H3', text:'The redundancy check'},
{t:'P', text:'Run this before the validation, because it is cheaper and can save you validating a specification you are going to change.'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Compute the correlation matrix among all seventeen rescaled metrics, using CONTROL and REFERENCE sites only — never the settlement contrast. Pay particular attention to VM13 against VM1 and VM2, and to VM14 against VM3 and VM5.',
 'Any two metrics correlating above 0.85: merge them, drop one, or record an explicit justification for keeping both.',
 'Any two DIMENSION scores correlating above 0.80: the index has fewer effective dimensions than it claims, and the aggregation needs reconsidering.',
]},
{t:'P', text:'What this prevents. If productivity is greenness-derived and structure is also substantially greenness-derived, then a site scoring highly on both has demonstrated one thing twice — and the geometric mean, chosen precisely to penalise imbalance, would register a spurious balance. The index would look robust while carrying little more information than its first component.'},

{t:'H2', text:'6.8  The site typology — classifying communities, not just averaging them'},
{t:'P', text:'Everything described so far produces ONE answer for the whole sample. That is the study’s primary finding, and it is not enough. These 212 communities will differ from each other enormously, and an average that hides that difference throws away the most interesting result in the dataset.'},
{t:'P', text:'But be careful about WHAT can be said of one community. The pooled estimate has a standard error of about 0.079 within-quartet standard deviations. A single community’s estimate has a standard error of 1.155 — FIFTEEN TIMES larger. For one community to be called "above its controls" at conventional confidence, its difference must be about ten times the pooled detectable difference. A RANKING of communities would therefore be mostly noise. A TYPOLOGY would not, because flag states rest on a two-component rule and dimension profiles are multivariate — both far more stable than a single number.'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Axis','Values','Where it comes from'],
 rows:[
 ['Direction','above / INDETERMINATE / below its matched controls','The shrinkage-adjusted per-site difference in VCI (§8.13). The middle class is the largest and it means "we cannot tell" — never "similar to controls".'],
 ['Maintenance mode','unflagged / water-subsidised / disturbance / both / unavailable','The two flags of §6.6, under the two-component rule.'],
 ['Dominant dimension','productivity / persistence / structure / configuration / balanced','Whichever of the four contributes most to the geometric mean relative to its stratum distribution. "Balanced" where no dimension leads by a declared margin.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'A community therefore carries a label such as "above-controls, unflagged, persistence-led" or "indeterminate, water-subsidised, productivity-led". Every component traces back to a measured quantity.'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['What a typology label is NOT',
  'A typology label describes a MEASURED PATTERN. It does not name a management system. "Persistence-led, unflagged" says the land holds cover through the season without evidence of imported water; it does not say the community mulches, or practises no-till, or is regenerative. This matters more in version 4.0 than it did in 3.8, because with the practice codes gone the typology is the most concrete per-community statement the study produces, and it will be read as a characterisation of the community if the wording lets it. It is a characterisation of the LAND.'],
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Four rules'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'RULE-BASED, NOT CLUSTERED. Fix the thresholds in advance and apply them mechanically. Clustering 212 communities on correlated features is unstable, and a grouping discovered after the scores exist is a specification search at the level of the classification. A rule also travels: a reader can apply it to a community your study never examined.',
 'EXPLORATORY. The typology carries no hypothesis and no test. It is described, not inferred from. It never moves into the confirmatory family.',
 'REPORT THE DISTRIBUTION, NOT A LEAGUE TABLE. How many communities fall in each combination, and how that distribution differs from the controls. Naming and ranking identifiable communities is where this becomes an ethical problem rather than a statistical one.',
 'THE MIDDLE CLASS IS "INDETERMINATE". Never merge it with "similar to controls". One is an absence of evidence, the other a positive finding of similarity, and at site level this study can rarely support the second.',
]},

// =========================================================================
{t:'H1', text:'Part 7  ·  The eight execution stages'},
{t:'P', text:'Work through these in order. Each produces something the next needs. Stages 1 and 3 can run in parallel; everything else is sequential.'},

{t:'H2', text:'Stage 1  ·  Essential documentary coding and measurement geometry'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['What this stage is now for, and why it was renamed',
  'Version 3.8 called this stage "Documentary coding" and it produced seven outputs, three of which were about practices. Version 4.0 names the stage after the two things it actually delivers: the ESSENTIAL DOCUMENTARY DATA the rest of the study consumes, and the MEASUREMENT GEOMETRY the whole study is measured over. Both are load-bearing and neither is optional. The stage was not deleted along with the practice codes — the polygon alone would justify it, and the onset dates carry two analyses.'],
]},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['BEFORE YOU START','Stage_1_Essential_Data_Workbook_v1. Complete Definitions_And_Freeze for every judgement-bearing variable, confirm every agreement threshold, and enter the freeze date. Draw the 20 per cent double-coding subsample with a recorded seed. Calibrate on communities NOT in the study sample and record it.'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO — the documentary half','Code all 212 communities from published sources, using the sixty-one fields of the web-search register v3.0. Record THREE candidate dates — formal founding, land acquisition, and the one that matters, ENVIRONMENTAL INTERVENTION ONSET — with an uncertainty band, an evidence rank and a confidence tier on the last. Code population, the documentary managed area with its band and basis, the five evidence channels, current status, and three context fields. Record the search itself: every address, every database consulted including those that returned nothing, and whether the run was truncated.'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO — the geometry half','Draw a polygon at every settlement by the procedure in §7.1, translate it onto all three controls, and export the lot. Use the documentary managed area to corroborate the drawing. Redraw a random 20 per cent after at least four weeks and report the overlap.'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO NOT DO','You do not code ecological practices. There is no practice matrix, no practice evidence sheet, no claim-to-signature mapping and no coding-level scale for practices anywhere in Stage 1 (§0.1). If a source describes a practice and the description also carries a DATE or an AREA, record the date or the area in its proper field and cite the source; the dated fact is what the study needs.'],
 ['WHAT YOU PRODUCE','The completed workbook: community register, onset register, polygon geometry, source index, source set, search log, disagreement log, reliability report, decisions log. Plus the exported polygon asset, ready for upload.'],
 ['DONE WHEN','All 212 rows complete; agreement reported per variable across the fourteen reliability variables; every coded value traceable to a source; every source address carrying a crawl status other than "not attempted"; A POLYGON DRAWN AND EXPORTED for every settlement with its area, confidence and reference-circle assignment; polygons translated onto all 636 controls; the 20 per cent redraw done and its overlap reported; and drawn-versus-stated area compared.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'7.1  Drawing the polygons — the most important manual step in the study'},
{t:'P', text:'This runs inside Stage 1, at every one of the 212 settlements and every cohort settlement. It takes ten to twenty minutes a site once you have the routine — roughly 60 to 90 hours across the sample — and it is what the whole measurement rests on.'},
{t:'P', text:'What you produce. One polygon per settlement, saved as a shapefile or GeoJSON, with a site_id matching your register. Its outline becomes the measurement area, its area picks the reference circle, and a copy of it — slid onto each control village — becomes the control geometry. You draw once and use it four times.'},
{t:'H3', text:'The procedure'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Open the site at its recorded coordinates in high-resolution imagery — Google Earth, Bing Maps or Esri World Imagery. Prefer imagery captured inside the study window; record the date and the source.',
 'Find the community centre: the cluster of buildings. This is the point your measurement zone will be drawn around, so confirm it matches the coordinates you hold and the coordinate_agreement value you coded.',
 'DRAW A POLYGON AROUND THE CONTIGUOUS MANAGED GROUND. Managed ground is anything showing deliberate human management — cultivated fields, orchards, planted blocks, garden beds, terraces, ponds, tracks, polytunnels, managed clearings, buildings and yards. Follow the real outline: if the land is a strip, draw a strip; if it wraps around a road, draw that shape.',
 'TRACE FROM STRUCTURE, NOT FROM COLOUR. Use field edges, tracks, fences, walls, hedgerows, terrace lines and abrupt changes in field pattern or texture. Do NOT use greenness. A ploughed brown field is exactly as managed as a green one, and a lush unmanaged thicket is not managed at all. This single rule is what keeps the measurement honest.',
 'Include bare and fallow ground that carries field structure. Exclude unmanaged surroundings: natural woodland with no visible management, open rangeland with no boundary structure, and neighbouring farms whose field pattern is clearly different.',
 'Exclude detached parcels more than 500 m from the centre. They cannot fall inside any measurement zone, so they are irrelevant even though they belong to the community. Where you do this, record it — it is the legitimate reason a documentary area can exceed a drawn one, and the parcel_structure field is what tells that case apart from a total-holding confusion.',
 'Measure the enclosed area in hectares with the imagery tool’s own area function.',
 'Record polygon_area_ha, the imagery date, the imagery source, and a drawing confidence of clear / moderate / poor. Export the polygon itself.',
 'MINIMUM SIZE 1.0 HECTARE. A polygon below that leaves roughly 56 usable pixels after built-up masking, which is near the 30-pixel floor and too fragile. Where the managed land is genuinely smaller, use a 75 m circle instead and flag the site.',
 'TRANSLATE the finished polygon onto each of the three control villages: slide it until its centre sits on the control centre. Do NOT resize, reshape or rotate it. Save each copy with the control’s site_id.',
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Five rules that keep it honest'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'DRAW FROM STRUCTURE, NEVER FROM COLOUR. Rule 4 above is the single most important instruction in this plan. A polygon defines the exact ground you measure. If you include the green corners and leave out the brown ones, that goes straight into your result — and it does NOT cancel, because the translated control polygon was not drawn with the same unconscious preference. It is one-directional and it inflates.',
 'DRAW BEFORE ANY INDEX VALUE EXISTS. No index value exists at Stage 1, so this is automatic — but do not be tempted to revisit a polygon after seeing a result. The geometry is frozen with the rest of the Stage 1 dataset.',
 'REDRAW a random 20 per cent after at least four weeks, without looking at your first polygons, and report the OVERLAP between the two drawings — the shared area divided by the combined area. Report the mean and how many fall below 0.80. This is the reliability statistic for the study’s primary measurement and the methods chapter needs it.',
 'NEVER DRAW A CONTROL’S OWN POLYGON. Controls receive their settlement’s shape, translated. Drawing a control its own outline would let you choose its land as well as the settlement’s, which is precisely the comparison this step must stay clear of.',
 'SC1 IS YOUR CHECK. It re-runs the whole analysis over EQUAL-AREA CIRCLES — a circle of the same area as the polygon, centred on the site centre. If the two agree, your drawing introduced no bias. If they differ, that difference is attributable to SHAPE alone, because area is held constant.',
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Corroborating with the documentary figure'},
{t:'P', text:'You now have two numbers per settlement: the documentary managed area from the coding, and the polygon area you just drew. The POLYGON DEFINES THE GEOMETRY in every case — it is a direct measurement of the ground. The documentary figure is the check, and it is the only external check that will ever exist on this measurement.'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Tier','Condition','Action'],
 rows:[
 ['A','Documentary area within 30% of the polygon area','The two agree. Record area_agreement_tier = A. Sensitivity check SC16 restricts to these.'],
 ['B','No documentary figure, OR the two differ by 30 to 100%','The polygon stands. Tier B, and keep both numbers. A SITE WITH A POLYGON AND NO DOCUMENTARY FIGURE IS TIER B, NEVER BLANK — a blank looks identical to a site nobody has coded yet.'],
 ['C','They differ by more than a factor of two','The polygon still stands, but investigate and record what you found in agreement_note.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'When they differ by more than a factor of two, look for two things. The commonest by far is that the documentary figure is the TOTAL HOLDING rather than the worked area — a community stating "our 200 hectares" while visibly working twelve; the polygon shows this at once and total_holding_ha is where the larger figure belongs. The second is a genuinely non-contiguous holding whose detached parcels the polygon excludes under rule 6 above; parcel_structure is what separates the two cases. Record what you found in the decisions log; it is also a finding about how communities describe their own land.'},
{t:'P', text:'Report the agreement between drawn and stated area across the whole sample as table T10, and the polygon redraw overlap as table T11. Nobody has published either comparison for this population, and together they are a small methodological contribution in their own right.'},

{t:'H2', text:'Stage 2  ·  Complete the control sample'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['BEFORE YOU START','Stage 1 complete (population, protected-area status and external-programme status all come from it). Your existing 212 conventional-rural controls.'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO','Add two more conventional-rural controls per settlement. Match on: identical Köppen main group; same biome; elevation within 300 m; same terrain class; DISTANCE TO PERMANENT SURFACE WATER within a declared tolerance; 5–50 km distant (see the ladder below); same country; classified rural; tree cover within 15 percentage points; population within a factor of three; NOT inside a protected area; NO documented external restoration programme. Rank surviving candidates on a weighted standardised distance and take the three closest. EVERY CONTROL RECEIVES ITS SETTLEMENT’S POLYGON, translated so the shape’s centre sits on the control village — same size, same shape, no rotation. Where nothing good exists, take the best available and FLAG it rather than dropping the settlement — silently dropping settlements without good local comparators would bias the sample toward regions rich in similar villages.'],
 ['THE DISTANCE LADDER','Worked down in order, stopping at the first step that yields three controls: (1) three controls at 5–50 km meeting every criterion, which is Tier 1; (2) fewer than three, so extend the search to 50–100 km and take the CLOSEST qualifying candidates rather than the best-scoring ones, which is Tier 2; (3) still fewer than three, so accept TWO controls at 5–50 km rather than a third from further out, Tier 2 and flagged; (4) fewer than two, so take the best available with a flagged tolerance breach, Tier 3; (5) NEVER drop the settlement. Record control_distance_km for every control. Step 2 comes before step 3 deliberately: three controls with one far gives a variance of 1.37 σ² while two nearby controls give 1.50, so the far third control is worth about nine per cent. Every criterion other than distance stays exactly as strict at every step — distance is relaxed precisely because the covariates do the real work. Relax both and the design is gone.'],
 ['WHAT YOU PRODUCE','A quartet register: 212 settlements × 3 controls = 848 study sites, each carrying its match-quality grade AND a polygon — drawn at the settlement, translated at the controls. Plus a Love plot (figure F4) and the three-tier grading below.'],
 ['DONE WHEN','Every settlement has three controls; the balance table and Love plot are produced; every quartet carries a match-quality tier; no covariate shows a standardised difference above 0.25 without being reported as imbalanced.'],
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Three-tier match quality — grade every quartet'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Tier','Definition','Use'],
 rows:[
 ['TIER 1 — close','Every continuous covariate within its declared tolerance, ALL THREE CONTROLS WITHIN 50 KM, and the weighted standardised distance below the declared acceptance threshold','The core sample. Sensitivity check SC5 restricts to these, which is also how the distance ladder is tested.'],
 ['TIER 2 — adequate','At most one covariate outside tolerance, OR one or more controls between 50 and 100 km, OR only two controls; weighted distance within 1.5× the threshold','Retained in the primary analysis, flagged. Report which of the three reasons applies.'],
 ['TIER 3 — best available','Distance above 1.5× the threshold; the settlement was kept rather than dropped because no better candidate existed','Retained and flagged. Report how many, and where they are — they cluster in regions with few comparable villages.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'The Love plot is the single most persuasive figure in a matched design. Plot the standardised mean difference for every covariate, before and after matching, on one horizontal axis with reference lines at 0.10 and 0.25. A reader sees in two seconds whether the matching worked. A balance table conveys the same information and almost nobody reads it.'},

{t:'H2', text:'Stage 3  ·  Draw the three reference pools'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['BEFORE YOU START','Nothing. This is independent of Stages 1 and 2 and should run alongside them.'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO','Draw random points across the global land surface with a RECORDED seed. Filter to the three pools using the criteria in §6.2. Add your existing 212 natural and 212 semi-urban sites to the first two pools. Split the semi-urban pool two-thirds / one-third with a second recorded seed. Target roughly 600 natural, 450 semi-urban and 300 productive, spread so that every stratum containing study sites has at least 30 sites of each relevant pool.'],
 ['WHAT YOU PRODUCE','A reference register with both seeds recorded and the pool membership of every site. Every reference site will be extracted at FIVE radii — 75, 110, 150, 210 and 300 m — so that each study site can be rescaled against a reference distribution computed on a unit close to its own polygon area (§4.3).'],
 ['DONE WHEN','Every stratum containing study sites has at least 30 natural and 30 productive reference sites AT EVERY RADIUS IT NEEDS, or is explicitly marked as pooled to a coarser rung. Record the rung per stratum per radius, since they can differ.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'Stage 3b  ·  Assemble the longitudinal cohort'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['BEFORE YOU START','Stage 1 coding under way, so that onset years are available. Decision on whether to proceed (see §11.3, decision D3).'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO','Enumerate communities whose environmental intervention onset falls in 2020 or 2021 — the CORE cohort. Communities with onset in 2019 may be added as an EXTENSION (§8.10). Use the same enumeration and the same web-search protocol as for the 212. Target 60–70 in the core. For each, record whether the onset is a FIRST intervention or a MAJOR NEW PROJECT at an established community, and either restrict to the first or report the two groups separately. Match three conventional-rural controls to each by the identical Stage 2 rules. Draw a polygon at each cohort settlement and translate it, exactly as for the 212. Verify centroids visually.'],
 ['WHAT YOU PRODUCE','A cohort register: ~65 settlements × 3 controls = ~260 sites, each with its onset year, onset confidence tier, first-or-major classification, match-quality tier, core-or-extension status, and its polygon.'],
 ['DONE WHEN','At least 50 cohort settlements with onset confidence tier A or B. BELOW 40, STOP AND DROP THE COMPONENT — an underpowered longitudinal test sitting beside the main result does more harm than leaving it out (§11.3).'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'Stage 4  ·  Earth Engine extraction'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['BEFORE YOU START','Stages 2, 3 and 3b complete, and your polygon asset uploaded to Earth Engine with site_ids matching the register. Part 4 open beside you.'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO','FOUR exports, not one. Export 1: the 848 study sites over 2019–2025, each over its POLYGON plus the five rings plus the common 150 m circle. Export 2: the ~260 cohort sites over 2017–2025, same geometry. Export 3: the 1,350 reference sites over 2019–2025 at ALL FIVE radii and no rings. Export 4, run later and kept in its own table: the 848 study sites and ~260 cohort sites over the EQUAL-AREA CIRCLE only, for sensitivity check SC1. Running them separately is what prevents a table in which some sites silently carry two extra years or the wrong zone radius. For every site, extract the six spectral indices as monthly composites, apply cloud masking, water masking and topographic correction, extract land-cover probabilities, canopy height, terrain and rainfall, and write BOTH masked and unmasked versions of every zone summary. Chunk each export by year and site block.'],
 ['WHAT YOU PRODUCE','Four tables — annual, static, climate and disturbance (§4.4) — plus the separate SC1 table.'],
 ['DONE WHEN','Every site has data for every year, or the absence has a recorded reason. The primary annual row count matches the arithmetic expectation of 105,182, and the SC1 table matches 8,276.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'Stage 5  ·  Data preparation'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['BEFORE YOU START','Stage 4 output, the Stage 1 workbook, the Stage 2 quartet register.'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO','Run steps DP1 to DP12 of Part 5 in order: masking, correction, compositing, vegetation-year alignment, annual metrics, quality screening, panel assembly, the six integrity checks, outlier screening, missing-data rules, then freeze the table.'],
 ['WHAT YOU PRODUCE','One frozen, versioned analysis table, plus a short assembly report recording the six integrity checks and the outlier resolutions.'],
 ['DONE WHEN','All six integrity checks pass. If a row count changed at any join, stop and find out why before going further.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'Stage 6  ·  Build and validate the index'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['BEFORE YOU START','The frozen analysis table from Stage 5.'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO','Compute reference levels per stratum per radius for each metric from the appropriate pool (§6.2). Rescale every metric, inverting the three that need it. Run the redundancy check on controls and reference sites. Aggregate into the four dimension scores, then VCI, PCI and MDS. Compute the two flags. THEN validate on the 40–60 known-answer landscapes, using the 70/30 split of §6.7, before touching your settlements.'],
 ['WHAT YOU PRODUCE','Rescaled metrics, dimension scores, VCI, PCI, MDS, LCC and both flags for every site. Plus a validation table, the redundancy matrix, and the typology thresholds of §6.8 fixed in advance.'],
 ['DONE WHEN','The validation passes: classes order correctly within each climate group, and a monoculture plantation scores below a balanced site of lower raw productivity. If not, fix the index NOW.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'Stage 7  ·  Analysis'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['BEFORE YOU START','Stage 6 complete and the index no longer being changed.'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO','Run placebo test PL1 FIRST — it costs one model fit and tells you whether the rest is worth reading. Then analyses A1 to A8 in order, then the per-community estimation of §8.13, then the eighteen sensitivity checks and the remaining two placebos.'],
 ['WHAT YOU PRODUCE','Results tables and figures as specified in Part 10, INCLUDING the per-community results table (§10.4) — which is both a thesis deliverable and the training data for the second study.'],
 ['DONE WHEN','Every hypothesis has an estimate with a confidence interval; every null carries its detectable difference; the per-community table is complete; all eighteen sensitivity checks and all three placebos are reported whether or not they change anything.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'Stage 8  ·  Writing'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['BEFORE YOU START','Stage 7 results. This stage can begin during Stage 5.'],
 ['WHAT YOU DO','Write the methods from Parts 3 to 6, the results from Part 8, the limitations from §10.3. Apply the language rules of §10.2 to every sentence, including the two rules added in v4.0 about practice attribution.'],
 ['WHAT YOU PRODUCE','The thesis chapter and the manuscript.'],
 ['DONE WHEN','Every limitation in §10.3 appears in your own words; the discontinued earlier study is disclosed in the methods; the exclusion of practice-level documentary evidence is stated in the methods as a design decision rather than left to the limitations; no sentence in the document fails the language substitution test.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'7.9  Why the measurement area is a drawn polygon, and what it costs'},
{t:'H3', text:'First, why the zone must be small'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Intervention size','Coverage of a 7.07 ha zone','Observable effect','Against a detectable difference of 0.020 NDVI'],
 rows:[
 ['1 ha','14%','0.014','UNDERPOWERED — 0.6×'],
 ['2 ha','28%','0.028','Detectable — 1.3×'],
 ['3 ha','42%','0.042','Comfortable — 1.9×'],
 ['5 ha','71%','0.071','Strong — 3.2×'],
 ['10 ha or more','100%','0.100','Strong — 4.5×'],
 ['5 ha at the old 500 m radius','6%','0.006','INVISIBLE — 0.3×'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'The last row is the discontinued study. Its null was produced by its measurement radius, not by the absence of an effect.'},
{t:'H3', text:'Second, why a circle is the wrong shape'},
{t:'TBL', header:['7 hectares of managed land, shaped as…','Area says','A 150 m circle really captures'],
 rows:[
 ['A compact block near the centre','fully covered','95%'],
 ['A rectangle, 350 × 200 m','fully covered','78%'],
 ['Two parcels either side of a road','fully covered','65%'],
 ['A valley strip, 700 × 100 m','fully covered','42%'],
 ['Terraces along a slope, 900 × 80 m','fully covered','38%'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'A polygon takes every row to 100 per cent, and a drawing accuracy of 90 to 95 per cent keeps nearly all of that. Even at 75 per cent accuracy — the worst case — a polygon beats a circle over any holding that is not a compact block. And it lowers noise rather than raising it: noise depends on how much ground is averaged, not on its shape, so a polygon of the same area is no noisier than a circle, while it removes something the circle carried — a variable amount of land the community does not manage, differing from site to site. That variability was pure noise.'},
{t:'P', text:'The one case where a circle wins is a small holding that already sat neatly inside it. Those are the sites where the circle was already doing its job, and SC1 will show it.'},
{t:'H3', text:'What all of this buys, and what it costs'},
{t:'P', text:'Shrinking the measurement zone from 500 m to community scale is what moved this study from impossible to comfortable. Drawing the real outline recovers a further 1.2 to 2.4 times at sites whose land is not a compact block, and roughly half the signal a circle was discarding at valley-strip and terraced sites.'},
{t:'P', text:'The costs are four, and all are paid rather than avoided. Sixty to ninety hours of drawing. Reference levels at five radii, which is most of the growth in the panel (§4.4). The configuration dimension moved to the common circle, because a polygon can be too small or too thin for a 70 metre pattern window (§6.5). And a bias risk larger than any circle-based design carried, because drawing defines the exact ground measured — controlled by the structural-drawing rule, the 20 per cent redraw, and SC1.'},

// =========================================================================
{t:'H1', text:'Part 8  ·  The analyses'},
{t:'P', text:'Eight named analyses, one per hypothesis. Each has a name you can use in the thesis, a specification, and a note on how to read the result. Version 3.8 had nine; A6, the claims-versus-delivery comparison, is deleted (§0.1) and the remainder are renumbered. Appendix D maps the old identifiers to the new.'},

{t:'H2', text:'8.1  The model form used by most of them'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'outcome  =  β₀  +  β₁·SITE_TYPE  +  β₂·built_fraction  +  Σ βₖ·covariates  +  quartet  +  ε'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'standard errors clustered at site level'},
{t:'P', text:'Read as: compare each settlement against its own three controls, adjusting for built-up fraction and the other covariates, and let each quartet have its own baseline. β₁ is the quantity of interest.'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Element','Why it is there'],
 rows:[
 ['Quartet as a fixed effect','Removes everything a settlement shares with its three controls — region, climate, biome, landscape, national policy. That is the largest source of variation in the data, so removing it measures every effect against a much smaller residual. This is free precision and the single most valuable feature of the matched design.'],
 ['Built fraction as a covariate','The largest confounder available. Explains about a fifth of the residual variation; including it improves the detectable difference by 10–20%.'],
 ['Clustering at site level','Observations within a site are not independent — they share soil, slope and management. Without clustering, standard errors are far too small and everything looks significant.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'8.2  The unit of analysis — stated explicitly'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['Which panel each analysis uses',
  'The PRIMARY outcome is the SEVEN-YEAR MEAN of the annual index, at SITE level. One row per site, 848 rows. This is what analyses A1, A2, A5 and A6 use. The SITE-YEAR panel — 848 sites × 7 years — is used only where the analysis is genuinely about variation over time: metric VM7 and both flag components FC1 and FC2, all of which are computed from the seven-year series and then enter the site-level table as single values, and sensitivity check SC14. The SITE-RING panel — 848 sites × 5 rings — is used only by analysis A3. The COHORT panel is separate throughout and is used only by A7. Mixing these gives different standard errors and different answers. Say in the methods which one each analysis uses.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'8.3  A1 — Matched-pair contrast on vegetation condition  [H1, PRIMARY]'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['Analysis name','Matched-pair contrast on vegetation condition'],
 ['Outcome','VCI, seven-year mean, over the site’s POLYGON — drawn at settlements, translated at controls (§7.1). Condition form, built-up masked. Configuration comes from the common 150 m circle at every site.'],
 ['Unit','Site. 848 rows.'],
 ['Specification','Linear model of VCI on SITE_TYPE with quartet fixed effects, built fraction, elevation, slope, tree cover, population and n_clear as covariates; standard errors clustered at site. Polygon area and shape need no adjustment here, because they are IDENTICAL within a quartet — settlement and controls share one shape by construction — so the quartet fixed effect absorbs them entirely.'],
 ['Estimand','β₁, the mean difference in VCI between a settlement and its matched controls, in index units.'],
 ['How to read it','A positive β₁ with an interval excluding zero means settlements hold better vegetation condition than their matched neighbours. Report β₁, its 95% interval, the detectable difference (0.22 SD), and n. It does NOT identify what the communities do to produce that difference (§3.8).'],
 ['Also report','The same model run separately on each of the four dimension scores, so a reader can see which dimensions carry the result. This is figure F6 and it matters: a result carried entirely by productivity and structure is exactly what site selection predicts.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'8.4  A2 — Equivalence test on provisioning capacity  [H2, PRIMARY]'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['Why this is an equivalence test and not an ordinary null test',
  'H2 claims that provisioning is NOT LOWER. Treating that as an ordinary null test — run the model, fail to reject, conclude no difference — is invalid: failing to find a difference is not evidence that none exists, and with enough noise any study can "prove" equivalence by being imprecise. The correct instrument is an EQUIVALENCE TEST with a declared margin, using two one-sided tests.'],
]},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['Analysis name','Equivalence test on provisioning capacity (two one-sided tests)'],
 ['Outcome','PCI, seven-year mean, over the site’s POLYGON, FULL zone unmasked.'],
 ['Equivalence margin','δ = 0.15 standard deviations, DECLARED IN ADVANCE. Justify it in the thesis as the smallest reduction in provisioning capacity that would materially qualify the claim.'],
 ['Specification','The same model as A1, with PCI as the outcome. Then test both one-sided hypotheses at the 5% level: that the difference is below −δ, and that it is above +δ.'],
 ['How to read it','If BOTH one-sided tests reject, provisioning is statistically equivalent within the margin, and H2 is supported. If the lower test fails to reject, a meaningful decline cannot be excluded — report that plainly rather than as "no difference".'],
 ['Reporting rule','Report the point estimate with its 90% interval (the interval that corresponds to two one-sided tests at 5%) alongside the margin, so a reader can see the comparison directly.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'8.5  A3 — Distance-interaction model  [H3]'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['Analysis name','Distance-interaction model (radial gradient)'],
 ['Outcome','VCI computed per ring, seven-year mean'],
 ['Unit','Site-ring. Up to 848 × 5 = 4,240 rows, fewer where a ring failed the 30-pixel gate.'],
 ['Specification','VCI ~ SITE_TYPE × RING + built fraction + covariates + quartet fixed effects, clustered at site. RING as an ordered factor.'],
 ['Estimand','The interaction terms. A settlement advantage that is LARGER in the inner rings than the outer ones is the spatial signature of something radiating from the settlement — and choosing a good parcel does not produce that pattern.'],
 ['IMPORTANT LIMITATION','This analysis covers THREE dimensions — productivity, persistence and structure — NOT four. The configuration metrics use a 70 m moving window, 140 m across, which is wider than three of the five rings; a per-ring configuration value would be a blend of its neighbours. Compute the ring-level index from VCI-P, VCI-S and VCI-T only, and state that in the thesis.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'8.6  A4 — Age-interaction model with penalised smooth  [H4]'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['Analysis name','Age-interaction model with penalised smooth'],
 ['Outcome','VCI, seven-year mean'],
 ['Specification','VCI ~ SITE_TYPE × s(ONSET_AGE) + covariates + quartet fixed effects, where s( ) is a penalised smooth. Controls carry their settlement’s onset age, so the control slope supplies the null.'],
 ['Why a smooth and not a line','Ecological signatures saturate. A straight line would understate a real relationship that flattens after twenty years, and would misreport its shape.'],
 ['Why the control slope matters','A settlement slope reported alone would be uninterpretable, because a common temporal trend across all sites would produce one. The estimand is the DIFFERENCE between the settlement and control slopes.'],
 ['Confounders to include and name','Founding decade (cohort), population (size) and DOCUMENTARY EVIDENCE TIER. Older communities differ from younger ones in more ways than age, and the three most obvious are named in the thesis rather than left implicit. The evidence tier is included here for a specific reason: onset dating quality correlates with documentation quality, which correlates with organisational capacity, which is the unmeasured confounder the causal diagram names.'],
 ['Sample restriction','Sites whose onset is flagged as a founding-year proxy, or whose onset could not be established, are excluded from A4 and from A4 only. Report how many.'],
 ['Onset uncertainty','Propagate the coded uncertainty band by multiple imputation across each community’s plausible onset range, so a community with a ±5-year band contributes less than one with a ±1-year band.'],
 ['Reporting language, fixed now','Report as "an observed pattern across communities of different ages", NEVER as "what happens to a community over time". This is a space-for-time substitution and its assumptions are only partly met.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'8.7  A5 — Restricted contrast on management-diagnostic metrics  [H5]'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['Analysis name','Restricted matched-pair contrast on the management-diagnostic score'],
 ['Outcome','MDS — the arithmetic mean of the SEVEN management-dominated rescaled metrics (§6.5)'],
 ['Specification','Identical to A1, with MDS as the outcome.'],
 ['Why this is not a rebuilt index','A four-dimension index rebuilt from M-class metrics only is impossible: productivity and structure contain no M-class metrics, so no four-dimension geometric mean can be formed. MDS is a single scale and is described as one.'],
 ['How to read it','Survival here is the strongest single answer to the site-selection objection. A parcel cannot be bought already carrying a long green season, a low bare-ground fraction at the trough, several vegetation types greening at different times, or a fine mosaic of small varied parcels. If the advantage appears in the full index but not in MDS, report it as consistent with site selection.'],
 ['What it does NOT show','A positive MDS result is management-consistent evidence. It is NOT evidence of any named practice, and the discussion must not name one (§3.8, §10.2).'],
 ['Also report','The seven metrics individually, so a reader can see whether MDS is carried by one metric or by several. Version 3.8 said six here and seven in its formula (§0.4, correction 1).'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'8.8  A6 — Density-matched comparison against held-out semi-urban sites  [H6]'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['Analysis name','Density-matched comparison against held-out semi-urban reference sites'],
 ['Sample','212 settlements against the HELD-OUT third of the semi-urban pool (§6.2), matched on built fraction within stratum.'],
 ['Why held-out','The reference two-thirds define the floor of the condition scale. Comparing settlements against those same sites would be circular — they score near zero by construction.'],
 ['Specification','Match each settlement to the nearest available held-out semi-urban site on built fraction within the same climate group and biome. Compare VCI with stratum fixed effects.'],
 ['How to read it','If settlements are indistinguishable from density-matched semi-urban sites, what the study measured is low density, not intentional design. If settlements exceed them, density alone does not account for the result.'],
 ['Honest note','This is among the least well powered analyses in the study (detectable difference around 0.40 SD, second only to A4 at 0.44) and the matching is on one variable only. Report it as a check on an alternative explanation, not as a finding in its own right.'],
 ['Note on numbering','This analysis was A7 in version 3.8. It is unchanged in substance.'],
]},

{t:'H2', text:'8.9  A7 — Pre-onset difference-in-differences  [H7]'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['What makes this different from every other analysis in the study',
  'Analyses A1 to A6 all compare settlements against controls at one moment and then argue that the difference is unlikely to be site selection. A7 observes the same land BEFORE and AFTER the intervention began. That changes what can be claimed. Each site is compared with ITSELF, so everything fixed about it — soil, slope, aspect, parcel history, whatever the founders saw in it — cancels out of the estimate. Site selection cannot produce a CHANGE at the moment of onset. And with three pre-onset years, the identifying assumption becomes TESTABLE. If settlements and their controls were already diverging before onset, you will see it. No other component of this study can check its own assumption. This analysis was A8 in version 3.8 and is unchanged in substance.'],
]},
{t:'H3', text:'The design — asymmetric windows, aligned to each community’s own onset'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['Why the before and after windows are NOT the same length',
  'Nothing in a difference-in-differences requires the two windows to match. Three years is ample to establish a baseline — the land was not changing, and two or three observations fix that. Three years is NOT ample to see the effect: fast-responding ground cover changes in one or two seasons, but pasture and grassland recovery takes two to five years, understory establishment three to four, and tree crown cover three to eight in humid climates and longer in dry ones. A three-year post-window measures the beginning of the change, not the change. This plan therefore uses THREE pre-onset years and FOUR post-onset years. The extra post-year raises the average effect by about a quarter while costing about six per cent in precision — a net gain of roughly a third.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'Each community’s windows are aligned to ITS OWN onset year rather than to the calendar. Event time is defined as the calendar year minus the onset year, so τ = −1 is the year before onset at every site regardless of when that was. The onset year itself (τ = 0) is dropped as a transition, because work beginning in March 2021 makes 2021 neither a clean before nor a clean after.'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Onset','Pre-period (τ −3…−1)','Skipped','Post-period (τ +1…+4)','Status'],
 rows:[
 ['2020','2017, 2018, 2019','2020','2021, 2022, 2023, 2024','CORE'],
 ['2021','2018, 2019, 2020','2021','2022, 2023, 2024, 2025','CORE'],
 ['2019','2017, 2018 only','2019','2020, 2021, 2022, 2023','EXTENSION — 2 pre-years only'],
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Why the core is 2020–2021 and not 2019 or 2022'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Onset range','Balanced window','Net gain vs 3/3','Assessment'],
 rows:[
 ['2020–2021','3 pre / 4 post','1.34× better','ADOPTED as the core. Best balance, and two pre-trend coefficients.'],
 ['2019–2020','2 pre / 5 post','1.46× better','Slightly better on paper, but only ONE pre-trend coefficient — and the pre-trend test is why this component exists.'],
 ['2019–2021','2 pre / 4 post','1.18× better','Worse than the core: the lost pre-year costs more than the gained post-year.'],
 ['2020–2022','3 pre / 3 post','baseline','Too short a post-window for anything but the fastest-responding cover changes.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'Three further reasons 2019 is an extension rather than the core. A 2019 onset needs 2017 as a pre-year, and Sentinel-2B only finished commissioning around the middle of 2017 — so half that year is single-satellite and thin, worst in the tropics. It gives only ONE pre-trend coefficient, which is a weak test of the assumption the whole component rests on. And the COVID pandemic would fall entirely inside its post-period, aligning a global shock exactly with the treatment timing, which is the worst possible arrangement for a differential confounder.'},
{t:'H3', text:'Specification'},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['Analysis name','Pre-onset difference-in-differences on the longitudinal cohort'],
 ['Sample','~65 CORE settlements with onset 2020–2021, each with 3 matched controls. ~260 sites. Plus any 2019-onset extension sites. SEPARATE from the 212 and never pooled with them. Admits onset confidence tier A and B only.'],
 ['Outcome','Δ VCI = (mean VCI over the FOUR post-onset years, τ +1 to +4) − (mean VCI over the THREE pre-onset years, τ −3 to −1). One value per site.'],
 ['Unit','Site. ~260 rows.'],
 ['Specification','Δ VCI ~ SITE_TYPE + built-fraction change + quartet fixed effects, clustered at site. The coefficient on SITE_TYPE is the difference-in-differences estimate.'],
 ['Estimand','The change in vegetation condition over four years following onset, at settlements, relative to the change over the same calendar years at their matched controls.'],
 ['Why this is more precise per site than A1','The site fixed effect cancels. The residual is no longer the between-site variation of 0.089 NDVI but the INTERANNUAL variation, which for a three-pre / four-post difference is roughly 0.015 to 0.031 — three to six times smaller. At 65 core sites that gives a detectable difference of about 0.006 to 0.012 NDVI, against an expected four-year effect of roughly 0.009 to 0.026.'],
 ['Rescaling note','Because rescaling is a linear transformation with stratum-constant anchors, the DiD estimate in rescaled units is simply the raw change divided by the stratum range. Rescale anyway, so that estimates from different strata can be pooled.'],
]},
{t:'H3', text:'The pre-trend test — the part that earns the component its place'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Fit an event-study model with a coefficient at every event time, using ALL available pre-years at each site — three for a 2020 onset, four for a 2021 onset, two for a 2019 extension site. The pre-trend test does not need a balanced panel, so it should use everything there is.',
 'EXPECT THE PRE-ONSET COEFFICIENTS TO BE ZERO. If settlements and controls were on parallel paths before onset, the parallel-trends assumption holds and the post-onset estimate can be read as the intervention.',
 'If a pre-onset difference is detectable, the assumption FAILS for this cohort. Report that plainly. It is the most damaging result the study can produce, and reporting it is the study working correctly rather than failing.',
 'LONG-HORIZON CHECK: on the subset with five or six post-onset years — the 2019 and 2020 onsets — estimate the effect at τ = +5 and τ = +6 as well. Report it as a secondary result with the changed composition stated.',
 'Plot every event time as figure F9, with the onset year marked, confidence bands throughout, and THE NUMBER OF CONTRIBUTING SITES LABELLED AT EACH POINT. Beyond τ = +4 the sample composition changes, and a reader must be able to see that the curve is no longer describing the same communities.',
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Three things to state in advance'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'The estimand differs from A1, and a null here does NOT undermine a positive A1. A1 measures the accumulated difference after a median of sixteen years. A7 measures the change over four years. If A1 finds 0.07 and A7 finds 0.01, those are consistent, not contradictory. Say so before you see the results, not afterwards.',
 'COVID is a real confound and DiD does not fully remove it. Onset in 2020–2021 means the post-period opens during a pandemic that drove unusual rural in-migration worldwide. A difference-in-differences removes shocks that hit settlements and controls equally. It does NOT remove a shock that hit intentional communities disproportionately — and in-migration to rural intentional communities plausibly was disproportionate. State this as a limitation and check whether the estimate differs between 2020-onset and 2021-onset communities.',
 'The cohort is a different population from the 212. Selected on recent onset, so these communities are younger, probably smaller and less established. Compare them against the 212 on population, country income group, climate group and evidence tier, and report the comparison (table T12). A7 speaks about recently founded communities; it does not automatically generalise to the whole sample.',
]},

{t:'H2', text:'8.10  A8 — Size-gradient model  [H8]'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['What makes this a real test rather than a fishing expedition',
  'The obvious question, once communities differ in size, is whether size itself changes how well land is held. The obvious analysis — compare small against large — is worthless, because a small community MUST show a smaller measured effect at a COMMON zone even if it manages its land exactly as well. Its work simply covers less of the ground being measured. But that dilution is exactly predictable: at a common 150 metre zone, a community working A hectares covers min(1, A / 7.07) of it. So the expected effect under a size-INDEPENDENT management quality is the local effect multiplied by that coverage — a curve that rises steeply to 7 hectares and is flat above it. A8 therefore does not ask "is there a size relationship?" — there must be one. It asks whether the observed relationship is STEEPER, FLATTER or EQUAL to what dilution alone predicts. That is a quantitative null, and it is what turns an obvious question into a testable one. This analysis was A9 in version 3.8.'],
]},
{t:'TBL', header:['',''],
 rows:[
 ['Analysis name','Size-gradient model against the dilution null'],
 ['Outcome','VCI on the COMMON 150 m zone, seven-year mean, condition form. NOT the polygon — see the note below.'],
 ['Sample','All settlements and controls. Every site has a polygon, so nothing is excluded for a missing area. n = 212 quartets.'],
 ['Unit','Site.'],
 ['Specification','VCI ~ SITE_TYPE × s(log POLYGON_AREA) + s(ONSET_AGE) + covariates + quartet fixed effects, where s( ) is a penalised smooth. Standard errors clustered at site.'],
 ['Estimand','The interaction: how the settlement–control difference changes with the area a community works.'],
 ['The comparison that matters','Plot the fitted interaction against polygon area, and OVERLAY the dilution curve — the local effect multiplied by min(1, A / 7.07 ha), scaled to the observed effect at large sizes. Figure F10.'],
 ['Why log area','Polygon areas span two orders of magnitude, from one hectare to over fifty. Untransformed, a handful of very large communities would determine the whole shape of the curve.'],
 ['Confounders to include and name','ONSET AGE above all — larger communities are usually older, so a size gradient can be an age gradient in disguise. Include the age smooth in the same model, and report both. Also population and country income group.'],
]},
{t:'H3', text:'Why A8 uses the COMMON zone and not the polygon'},
{t:'P', text:'This is the single most important design decision in the analysis, and getting it wrong would make the result meaningless. If A8 ran on the polygons, a small community would be measured over 2 hectares and a large one over 25 — so any difference between them would be partly the measurement scale rather than the communities. The estimand would differ by group, which is exactly the confound the analysis exists to avoid. On the COMMON 150 metre zone every site is measured identically, the dilution curve is exactly computable, and the comparison is clean. The common zone is extracted for every site anyway (§4.3), so this costs nothing.'},
{t:'H3', text:'How to read the four possible outcomes'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Result','What it means'],
 rows:[
 ['The observed gradient FOLLOWS the dilution curve','Management quality is size-independent. Small communities work their land as well as large ones, and their smaller measured effect is purely an artefact of measuring over ground they do not manage. A clean, useful and quite likely finding.'],
 ['The observed gradient is STEEPER than dilution predicts','Larger communities hold their land better per hectare. Check hard that it is not the age gradient in disguise before reporting it as a size effect.'],
 ['The observed gradient is FLATTER than dilution predicts','Smaller communities do better per hectare, consistent with concentrated effort on a small area. The least expected of the three and the most interesting if it holds.'],
 ['No gradient and no main effect','There is nothing to explain. Read it with A1 rather than on its own.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'One honest limitation. Polygon area is a direct measurement rather than a self-report, so it carries far less error than a coded managed area would. Some error remains — the redraw overlap in table T11 measures it — and error in a predictor attenuates a relationship toward flat. A8 may therefore slightly UNDERSTATE a true size gradient. Report the redraw overlap beside a flat result. Version 3.8 additionally reported reduced sample sizes here "where managed area is uncoded"; that no longer applies and never should have, because the polygon reaches every site (§0.2).'},

{t:'H2', text:'8.11  Detectable differences, per hypothesis'},
{t:'P', text:'These come from the pilot’s measured residual variation of 0.089 NDVI units at a 150 m radius, and from d = sqrt(10.47 / n) with three controls per settlement. At n = 212 that gives 0.222 SD. Report the relevant one with every null result.'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Analysis','What it tests','Detectable difference','Note'],
 rows:[
 ['A1','Condition, matched contrast','0.22 SD ≈ 0.020 NDVI','The study’s best-powered test.'],
 ['A2','Provisioning, equivalence','0.22 SD ≈ 0.020 NDVI','Against a declared margin of 0.15 SD.'],
 ['A3','Ring interaction','0.36 SD ≈ 0.032 NDVI','Interactions cost precision.'],
 ['A4','Age interaction','0.44 SD ≈ 0.040 NDVI','The weakest of the secondary tests, and further attenuated by onset dating error. A 7-year coding error roughly doubles the sample needed. Report the achieved n after the proxy-onset exclusion.'],
 ['A5','Management-diagnostic score','0.22 SD ≈ 0.020 NDVI','Same power as A1 — it is the same contrast on a different scale.'],
 ['A6','Density comparison','0.40 SD ≈ 0.035 NDVI','1:1 matching against ~150 held-out sites.'],
 ['A7','Pre-onset difference-in-differences','0.40 SD ≈ 0.006–0.012 NDVI','Standardised MDE is similar to A6, but the NDVI-unit figure is much SMALLER because the residual is interannual rather than between-site variation. Detectable against an expected four-year effect of 0.009–0.026 — real, and able to fail.'],
 ['A8','Size gradient','0.44 SD at n = 212','A smooth interaction, so it carries the same penalty as A4. Every site has a polygon, so n is 212 with no attrition for a missing area.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'Version 3.8 carried a ninth row here, for the claims-versus-delivery comparison at 0.39 SD, and a set of reduced-n figures for the size gradient. Both are deleted (§0.2).'},

{t:'H2', text:'8.12  Per-community estimation — the shrinkage step'},
{t:'P', text:'Analyses A1 to A7 answer questions about the sample. This section answers a different question: what can be said about ONE community? It is needed for two reasons — the typology of §6.8 requires a per-site direction, and the second study of this thesis cannot be trained without per-site values.'},
{t:'P', text:'The precision problem, with its numbers. The pooled estimate has a standard error of about 0.079 within-quartet standard deviations at n = 212. A single community’s estimate has a standard error of 1.155 — fifteen times larger. For one community to be classed above its controls at conventional confidence, its difference must exceed about 2.26 standard deviations, roughly ten times the pooled detectable difference. A raw per-site difference is therefore a very noisy quantity, and reporting 212 of them without correction would produce a table in which the extremes are mostly sampling error. The standard remedy is shrinkage.'},
{t:'P', text:'Fit a hierarchical model in which each community’s difference is drawn from a common distribution. Each site’s reported estimate then becomes a weighted blend of its OWN measured value and the sample mean, with the weight determined by how precisely that site was measured. A well-observed site keeps more of its own value; a noisy one is pulled further toward the middle.'},
{t:'FORMULA', text:'weight on the site’s own value  =  τ²  /  ( τ² + σᵢ² )'},
{t:'TBL', header:['Between-site variance','Keeps of its own value','Meaning'],
 rows:[
 ['0.5 σ²','27%','Communities differ little; individual estimates are mostly noise and are pulled hard toward the mean.'],
 ['1.0 σ²','43%','Moderate real variation between communities.'],
 ['2.0 σ²','60%','Substantial real variation; individual estimates carry real information.'],
 ['4.0 σ²','75%','Communities differ a great deal; shrinkage barely moves them.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'The realised weight is estimated from the data rather than assumed, and it is itself a reportable finding: a high weight means the communities genuinely differ from one another, which is worth knowing independently of whether they differ from their controls.'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Deposit BOTH the raw and the shrunk estimate for every community, so a reader can see how far each moved and why.',
 'Use the SHRUNK values for the typology and for anything the second study trains on. Use the raw values only for the diagnostic above.',
 'Attach an interval to every per-community value. A point estimate alone invites exactly the over-reading the shrinkage exists to prevent.',
 'Never publish a ranked table of named communities. Deposit under anonymous codes and keep the key yourself (§10.4).',
]},

{t:'H2', text:'8.13  Multiplicity, and what not to do'},
{t:'BOX', rows:[
 ['Recalculated for version 4.0, not carried forward',
  'Version 3.8 stated that "H1 and H2 are primary and tested without correction; H3 to H9 form one family and are corrected within it using false discovery rate control" — a family of SEVEN. It also ran a SECOND, nested multiplicity correction inside A6, across the practice codes that passed the prevalence rule. Version 4.0 has ONE family of SIX, and no nested correction anywhere.'],
]},
{t:'P', text:'H1 and H2 are PRIMARY and are tested at the conventional level without correction. They are two pre-specified tests of one two-part claim, and neither is a substitute for the other.'},
{t:'P', text:'H3, H4, H5, H6, H7 and H8 form ONE family of six SECONDARY hypotheses and are corrected within it using false discovery rate control. Report raw and adjusted values for all six.'},
{t:'P', text:'A note on H7, which is the one arguable member. The cohort analysis sits on a disjoint sample with a different estimand, and a case could be made for treating it as its own family. It is kept inside the secondary family because it is a pre-declared confirmatory test in the same study, and because the more conservative choice is the right one when the alternative would look like carving out the test most likely to produce a clean result. The composition of the family is declared here, before any result exists, and does not change afterwards.'},
{t:'P', text:'Removing one member from a six-test family makes the false discovery rate adjustment marginally less severe than it was over seven. That is a real if small consequence of the deletion and it is stated rather than left to be noticed.'},
{t:'P', text:'Everything not in the list above is EXPLORATORY: the typology, the per-community table, the dimension breakdown, the land-cover composition comparison, and every one of the eighteen sensitivity checks and three placebo tests. Exploratory results are labelled as such and never move up.'},
{t:'NUM', items:[
 'Do not add covariates until something becomes significant. The list in §3.7 is the list. You may REMOVE one that turns out collinear, and you note that you did.',
 'Do not report observed power. It is a restatement of the p-value and carries no information. Report the detectable difference, which does not depend on what you found.',
 'Do not promote an exploratory finding. Report it as exploratory and name it as a question for future work.',
 'Do not average VCI and PCI. The whole claim is that both hold at once.',
 'Do not reintroduce a deleted variable after seeing the results. The practice codes are removed for the reasons in §0.1, and those reasons do not change when a result is disappointing.',
]},

];
